<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <!--
            allowBackup is false, and deliberately.

            Android's automatic backup copies an application's private storage -
            here, the OPFS directory holding the entire SQLite database - to the
            user's Google Drive. For an application whose stated promise is that
            data never leaves the device, leaving that on would make the promise
            false, quietly, by default, on the first idle overnight charge.

            The cost is that a new phone does not inherit the data. That cost is
            already covered: Settings has an explicit backup export that writes
            a file the user chooses to keep, and an import that reads it back.
            A backup you performed is worth more than one you assumed.

            The AI assistant does not soften this. A question may travel; the
            database still has no route to Google Drive or anywhere else.
        -->

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!--
        Permissions requested from the system: one, and this comment used to
        say none.

        That sentence was true until the AI assistant. A person pastes their
        own Anthropic key into Settings and asks a question; answering it means
        reaching https://api.anthropic.com, and on Android nothing reaches
        anything without INTERNET. The WebView's request fails before it
        resolves a name. There is no per-host form of the permission, no way to
        hold it only while a feature is switched on, and no way to hand a
        release build a key it can use without one. So it is declared, once,
        below.

        What used to be proved by its absence is now proved in three narrower
        places, each of which still fails closed:

          - index.html allows exactly one host, and connect-src refuses the
            rest at the browser.
          - scripts/audit-offline.mjs allows that host in exactly one module,
            src/services/ai/client.ts, and fails the build on any other
            external URL anywhere.
          - With no key stored, and with the assistant switched off, nothing
            runs and nothing is sent. That is the shipping default.

        RECORD_AUDIO is not here and never will be. The microphone was removed
        outright: Android's recognizer refuses EXTRA_PREFER_OFFLINE with no
        offline Portuguese pack installed, which is this phone, so voice
        commands were dropped rather than left to fail in silence. Reading
        answers aloud stays, and speech synthesis asks for nothing. Do not add
        it: the check in .github/workflows/android.yml fails on it.

        That check was narrowed rather than deleted. It allows exactly
        android.permission.INTERNET and still fails the build on every other
        permission outside this application's own namespace: RECORD_AUDIO,
        CAMERA, location, contacts, storage, anything a later dependency drags
        in. It used to prove "this application asks the system for nothing". It
        now proves "this application asks for the network and nothing else",
        which is a weaker sentence and still worth checking on every build,
        because the way this promise would actually be lost is one library
        quietly merging one more permission.

        The merged manifest also carries one entry added by androidx:
        DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION, namespaced under this
        application's own id. It is a permission this application defines to
        keep other applications away from its broadcast receivers, not one it
        asks anybody for. The workflow allows that name and fails on any other.

        None of this changes how the application is loaded. Capacitor serves it
        from https://localhost through WebViewAssetLoader, which reads the
        APK's own assets and never opens a socket. Every asset is still inside
        the package, and the first launch still works with the radio off.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

</manifest>
